  <tr><td>Guardianship and custody orders</td><td>Appeal under Section 19(1)</td><td>Division Bench, Delhi High Court</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Maintenance under Section 125 CrPC / Section 144 BNSS</td><td>Revision preserved by Section 19(4)</td><td>Single Judge, Delhi High Court</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Consent decree (including mutual consent divorce)</td><td>No appeal — Section 19(2)</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Interlocutory orders</td><td>No appeal under Section 19(1)</td><td>Limited supervisory remedies, where maintainable</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What a Section 19 appeal can actually achieve</h2>
<p>Unlike a second appeal in civil litigation, the Section 19(1) appeal is a full first appeal: the Division Bench re-examines both facts and law. Findings on cruelty, desertion or the welfare of a child are all open. That width cuts both ways — an appellant can attack the Family Court&rsquo;s appreciation of evidence, but the respondent can support the decree on grounds the trial court rejected. Section 19(6) requires the appeal to be heard by a Bench of two judges, which is why these matters are listed before Division Benches.</p>

<h2>Building the appeal: the working sequence</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Day 0&ndash;3: certified copy.</strong> Apply immediately. The thirty-day clock under Section 19(3) is unforgiving, and the copy application date anchors the exclusion of time.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Week 1: scope decision.</strong> Identify whether the challenge is to findings of fact, legal error, or both; an appeal that attacks everything persuades on nothing.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Week 2&ndash;3: the paper book.</strong> Memorandum of appeal with grounds, certified copy of the judgment and decree, trial court record references, and any application for stay or interim arrangements — particularly where custody, residence or ongoing maintenance will be affected during the appeal&rsquo;s pendency.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Filing and listing.</strong> Registry scrutiny, removal of objections, and first listing — where interim protection, if sought, is usually argued.</div>
</div>

<h2>Three traps seen in practice</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>&bull; <strong>Treating the maintenance order as appealable.</strong> A Family Court order under Section 144 BNSS goes up in revision, not appeal — and revisional scope is narrower, so the grounds must be framed as jurisdictional or perversity-based, not as a plea to re-weigh evidence.</p>
  <p>&bull; <strong>Sitting on limitation because settlement talks continue.</strong> Negotiation does not stop the thirty days. File, then talk.</p>
  <p>&bull; <strong>Ignoring the decree&rsquo;s operation in the meantime.</strong> An appeal does not automatically stay the decree. Where remarriage, property transfers or relocation of a child could follow the decree, interim orders must be sought at the first listing, not after the fact.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This explainer describes the general framework under the Family Courts Act, 1984 as applied in Delhi. It is general information, not legal advice; the maintainability of any particular challenge depends on the specific order and the current case law.</div>
